---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="What the Badminton Queue App is and what this Digital Twin is.">
  <PageHeader
    title="About"
    subtitle="What this app does, and what this documentation site is."
  />

  <div class="px-8 py-10 max-w-2xl space-y-10">

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-3">What is the Badminton Queue App?</h2>
      <div class="text-sm text-ink-3 space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The Badminton Queue App is a real-time session management tool for badminton gym sessions.
          An organizer creates a session, players join anonymously via QR code, and the app automatically
          assembles fair 2v2 matches from the waiting queue.
        </p>
        <p>
          The matchmaking engine balances three competing concerns: <span class="text-ink-2">wait fairness</span>
          (players who've waited longer get higher priority), <span class="text-ink-2">skill balance</span>
          (pairs are capped at a 2-level spread), and <span class="text-ink-2">variety</span>
          (anti-repeat logic ensures you're not playing with the same people every match).
        </p>
        <p>
          When a match finishes, scores are submitted by any player on the court, the court frees up,
          and the engine automatically drafts the next match. The organizer can review, edit, and publish
          draft matches before players are notified — this is <em>Draft Mode</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          At the end of a session, every player gets a <em>Session Wrapped</em> screen with their stats,
          win streak, head-to-head records, and awards.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-3">What is this Digital Twin?</h2>
      <div class="text-sm text-ink-3 space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          This site is an interactive architecture reference — a "living document" that auto-updates
          when the source files change. It visually explains every layer of the codebase: the database
          schema, server action surface, matchmaking engine pipeline, Supabase Realtime fan-out pattern,
          and component hierarchy.
        </p>
        <p>
          The headline feature is <a href="/flows" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hi transition-colors underline underline-offset-2">Flows & Traces</a>
          — 11 animated sequence diagrams that let you pick a user gesture and trace it from tap, through
          the server action, into Postgres, through Realtime, and back to the re-rendering UI component.
        </p>
        <p>
          It was built by <span class="text-ink-2 font-medium">nayzjuan</span> as a personal architectural
          reference, but designed to be shareable with anyone curious about how the app works.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-3">Tech stack</h2>
      <div class="dt-card">
        <table class="dt-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Tool</th><th>Why</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {[
              ['Framework',   'Astro 5',                      'Static-first with per-page islands. Docs sites should not ship JS for static content.'],
              ['Styling',     'Tailwind CSS v4',               'CSS-first config via @theme. No tailwind.config.js. Matches host app.'],
              ['Diagrams',    'Mermaid 11',                    'Sequence, flowchart, and state diagrams with a consistent dark theme.'],
              ['Search',      'Pagefind',                      'Build-time search index. No server. Works offline.'],
              ['Code',        'Shiki (github-dark-dimmed)',    'Build-time syntax highlighting. Zero runtime cost.'],
              ['Font',        'Barlow Semi Condensed + Geist + Geist Mono', 'Display headings + precise body + cohesive code family.'],
              ['Extraction',  'chokidar + tsx',               'Watches host app source files and regenerates manifest.json on save.'],
            ].map(([layer, tool, why]) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="text-xs text-ink-3">{layer}</td>
                <td class="text-xs font-semibold text-accent font-mono">{tool}</td>
                <td class="text-xs text-ink-3">{why}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-3">Source</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-3">
        The host application lives at
        <a
          href="https://github.com/nayzjuan/badminton-app"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="text-accent hover:text-accent-hi transition-colors underline underline-offset-2"
        >
          github.com/nayzjuan/badminton-app ↗
        </a>.
        This Digital Twin lives in the <code class="font-mono text-accent text-xs">digital-twin/</code>
        subdirectory of the same repository.
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>
